---
import NewTabLink from '../actions/NewTabLink.astro'
import Band from '../band/Band.astro'
import SectionHead from '../band/SectionHead.astro'
import FaqItem from '../marketing/FaqItem.astro'

const DOCS = 'https://docs.dumdummisha.ai'
---

<Band id="faq" width={820}>
  <SectionHead eyebrow="FAQ" title="Questions, answers first." />
  <div class="dd-faq">
    <FaqItem question="Do I need to use Slack?" isOpen>
      For now, yes. I'm still working on adapters for Microsoft Teams and Discord.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Do I need a Slack app or admin approval?">
      No Slack app, so there's nothing for a workspace admin to approve. A browser extension lets me read and post in
      the Slack tab you're already signed in to, with your own session. Slack's terms and your workplace's rules for
      tools and AI still apply. <NewTabLink href={`${DOCS}/understand/how-it-works#without-a-slack-app`}>How that works</NewTabLink>.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Where do my messages and code go?">
      I run on your computer. There's no Dumdum Misha server. What your work reaches doesn't change. The messages, code
      and tool results I work with go to Anthropic under your own account, the same as when you use Claude Code yourself,
      and to OpenAI only if you run me or my workers on GPT. <NewTabLink href={`${DOCS}/privacy`}>The privacy policy</NewTabLink>
      lists every recipient.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Are you going to replace me?">
      No, unless you want me to. My main job is to take the routine part and bring you the decisions I can't safely
      settle. And if you decide to YOLO, then <em>you</em> are still in control. No multi-billion-dollar company is
      behind me. Michal created me specifically to reclaim his own time.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Are your replies marked?">
      By default, yes. I post through your Slack account, and my replies start with my emoji and are in italics, so
      colleagues can tell. Stealth mode drops both if you'd rather the marker didn't show.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="What can my colleagues ask you?">
      Anything they'd ask you!
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Can someone trick you into breaking your rules?">
      I've been tested by working alongside real people in a professional environment, where plenty of them tried to
      talk me out of my rules. That doesn't make me trick-proof, but I'm battle-tested.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Can I see what you'd send before it goes out?">
      Yes, and a new setup starts that way. Every reply, reaction and file waits in my window for your OK, and so does
      any work a message would start. Approve it, decline it or tell me what to change, and switch either check off once
      you trust me. To stop me altogether, pause me. I stop watching Slack and stop my workers, I don't catch up on
      messages that arrive while I'm paused, and your own Claude Code and OpenCode sessions keep working.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Which AI do you use?">
      Claude Code by default, your own unmodified Claude Code on your Claude subscription. Or OpenCode, and I answer
      with any provider it supports, GPT included, without needing Codex. Bigger jobs go to workers in either one, each
      on the model and effort the job needs, so agents from different providers can work on one problem together. <NewTabLink href={`${DOCS}/understand/how-it-works`}>How it fits together</NewTabLink>.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="What do you cost?">
      Nothing. But can Michal pay for swimming lessons out of this in the future? 🥺
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Is this only for developers?">
      No, though the pilot starts with software teams. A PM can ask me what a ticket leaves out of scope, a designer can
      have me read a Figma screen, and anyone can ask what was said in a meeting without touching code. You write my routines
      yourself, so they can cover product or design work as well as engineering. The aim is a broader personal
      assistant.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Who is behind Dumdum Misha?">
      <NewTabLink href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/michaljarnot">Michal Jarnot</NewTabLink
      >, a software engineer with a decade of experience building products for Silicon Valley companies. He built me
      because he needed one.
    </FaqItem>
  </div>
</Band>
